ticket_data.description:
#37436 made `FETCH_PEERS` tolerate peers whose rows were deleted, but only for deferred fields. Related objects still have two problems.

=== 1. A deleted peer makes other peers fail ===

Models:

{{{#!python
from django.contrib.contenttypes.fields import GenericForeignKey
from django.contrib.contenttypes.models import ContentType
from django.db import models


class Author(models.Model):
    name = models.CharField(max_length=100)


class Book(models.Model):
    title = models.CharField(max_length=100)
    author = models.ForeignKey(Author, models.CASCADE)


class Tag(models.Model):
    content_type = models.ForeignKey(ContentType, models.CASCADE)
    object_id = models.PositiveIntegerField()
    content_object = GenericForeignKey()
}}}

{{{#!python
book1, book2 = Book.objects.only("title").fetch_mode(FETCH_PEERS).order_by("pk")
Book.objects.filter(pk=book1.pk).delete()  # e.g. by another request
book2.author.name
}}}

`book2` and its author both exist, but this raises the error of the deleted peer:

{{{
Book.DoesNotExist: Book matching query does not exist.
}}}

Every later access raises it again, because the deleted peer stays in the peer list. With `FETCH_ONE`, `book2.author.name` returns `'Bob'`. A `GenericForeignKey` behaves the same way: `tag2.content_object` raises `Tag.DoesNotExist`.

Cause: `fetch_many()` on `ForwardManyToOneDescriptor` and `GenericForeignKeyDescriptor` passes all peers to `prefetch_related_objects()`. The prefetch reads the deferred `author_id` of the deleted peer, and `DeferredAttribute` raises `DoesNotExist` for it.

#37436 says "The missing peer should not cause an unrelated peer's deferred-field access to fail." The same should hold for related objects.

=== 2. A non-null foreign key returns None ===

{{{#!python
