raw_data.Description:
#37436 made `FETCH_PEERS` tolerate peers whose rows were deleted, but only for deferred fields. Related objects still have two problems.

=== 1. A deleted peer makes other peers fail ===

Models:

{{{#!python
from django.contrib.contenttypes.fields import GenericForeignKey
from django.contrib.contenttypes.models import ContentType
from django.db import models


class Author(models.Model):
    name = models.CharField(max_length=100)


class Book(models.Model):
    title = models.CharField(max_length=100)
    author = models.ForeignKey(Author, models.CASCADE)


class Tag(models.Model):
    content_type = models.ForeignKey(ContentType, models.CASCADE)
    object_id = models.PositiveIntegerField()
    content_object = GenericForeignKey()
}}}

{{{#!python
book1, book2 = Book.objects.only("title").fetch_mode(FETCH_PEERS).order_by("pk")
Book.objects.filter(pk=book1.pk).delete()  # e.g. by another request
book2.author.name
}}}

`book2` and its author both exist, but this raises the error of the deleted peer:

{{{
Book.DoesNotExist: Book matching query does not exist.
}}}

Every later access raises it again, because the deleted peer stays in the peer list. With `FETCH_ONE`, `book2.author.name` returns `'Bob'`. A `GenericForeignKey` behaves the same way: `tag2.content_object` raises `Tag.DoesNotExist`.

Cause: `fetch_many()` on `ForwardManyToOneDescriptor` and `GenericForeignKeyDescriptor` passes all peers to `prefetch_related_objects()`. The prefetch reads the deferred `author_id` of the deleted peer, and `DeferredAttribute` raises `DoesNotExist` for it.

#37436 says "The missing peer should not cause an unrelated peer's deferred-field access to fail." The same should hold for related objects.

=== 2. A non-null foreign key returns None ===

{{{#!python
book1, book2 = Book.objects.fetch_mode(FETCH_PEERS).order_by("pk")
Author.objects.filter(pk=book2.author_id).delete()
book2.author
}}}

`FETCH_PEERS` returns `None`, although `author` isn't nullable. `FETCH_ONE` raises `Author.DoesNotExist`. `prefetch_related()` raises `RelatedObjectDoesNotExist`. `ForwardManyToOneDescriptor.__get__()` returns right after `fetch()` and skips its `not self.field.null` check.

=== Possible fix ===

In `fetch_many()`, skip peers whose own row is gone (their local related value raises `DoesNotExist`). In `__get__()`, run the null check after fetching too. I have a draft patch with tests and can submit it if this is accepted.

ticket_data.description:
Split from #37508.

With `FETCH_PEERS`, a non-null foreign key returns `None` when the related row no longer exists. `FETCH_ONE` and `prefetch_related()` both raise an exception instead.

Models:

{{{#!python
from django.db import models


class Author(models.Model):
    name = models.CharField(max_length=100)


class Book(models.Model):
    title = models.CharField(max_length=100)
    author = models.ForeignKey(Author, models.CASCADE)
}}}

{{{#!python
book1, book2 = Book.objects.fetch_mode(FETCH_PEERS).order_by("pk")
Author.objects.filter(pk=book2.author_id).delete()  # e.g. by another request
book2.author
}}}

Results:

{{{
FETCH_PEERS       -> None
FETCH_ONE         -> Author.DoesNotExist: Author matching query does not exist.
prefetch_related  -> Book.author.RelatedObjectDoesNotExist: Book has no author.
}}}

`author` isn't nullable, so `None` breaks the descriptor's contract. Code like `book.author.name` fails with an `AttributeError` on `None` instead of a `DoesNotExist` that callers can catch.

Cause: `ForwardManyToOneDescriptor.__get__()` returns right after `instance._state.fetch_mode.fetch(self, instance)`. That skips the `rel_obj is None and not self.field.null` check that runs on the cached path. `prefetch_related()` takes the cached path, which is why it raises.

Fix: assign the fetched value to `rel_obj` and fall through to the null check. A nullable foreign key would still return `None`, as `prefetch_related()` does.

