ticket_data.description:
`Query.names_to_path()` accepts an annotation name at any position in a lookup path. A name after a relation should resolve on the related model, but if the base query has an annotation with that name, Django uses the annotation's output field instead. When that field is a model field, the query compares the related table's column of the same name.

The `_filtered_relations` branch next to it already checks `pos == 0`; the annotation branch does not.

Models:

{{{#!python
class Publisher(models.Model):
    name = models.CharField(max_length=255)


class Book(models.Model):
    isbn = models.CharField(max_length=9)
    name = models.CharField(max_length=255)
    publisher = models.ForeignKey(Publisher, models.CASCADE)
}}}

Reproduction:

{{{#!python
>>> qs = Book.objects.annotate(other_name=F("name"))
>>> print(qs.filter(publisher__other_name="Apress").query)
SELECT ... FROM "app_book" INNER JOIN "app_publisher"
ON ("app_book"."publisher_id" = "app_publisher"."id")
WHERE "app_publisher"."name" = Apress
}}}

Publisher has no field `other_name`, yet the query runs and filters on `Publisher.name`, so it returns wrong rows. If the related model has no column of that name, the database raises an error instead:

{{{#!python
>>> list(Book.objects.annotate(other_isbn=F("isbn")).filter(publisher__other_isbn="1"))
OperationalError: no such column: app_publisher.isbn
}}}

The same happens with `exclude()`, `order_by("publisher__other_name")` and `F("publisher__other_name")`. On main it also happens in `values("publisher__other_name")` and with `alias()` instead of `annotate()`.

Expected: `FieldError`, as 6.1 raises for the `alias()` and `values()` cases:

{{{
FieldError: Unsupported lookup 'other_name' for ForeignKey or join on the field not permitted.
FieldError: Cannot resolve keyword 'other_name' into field. Choices are: ...
}}}

Versions:

|| || stable/6.1.x || main ||
|| `annotate()` + `filter()`, `exclude()`, `order_by()`, `F()` || wrong SQL || wrong SQL ||
|| `annotate()` + `values()` || FieldError || wrong SQL ||
|| `alias()` + `filter()`, `exclude()`, `order_by()`, `values()`, `F()` || FieldError || wrong SQL ||

The `annotate()` cases in the first row date from 1f7b25c1a7a85426675a04380f37b180edc08bbc (#24986, Django 1.9). The other rows are a regression in be6cf832293779d8aeaeddca8c47a37ba1530898 (#37274), which changed the check from `self.annotation_select` to `self.annotations`. That commit is on main only.

Suggested fix: match annotation names only at the start of the path, as the filtered relation branch does:

{{{#!diff
-                if name in self.annotations:
+                if pos == 0 and name in self.annotations:
}}}

I have a patch with tests ready. Django's PR limit stops me from opening a PR while my earlier ones ([https://github.com/django/django/pull/21642 PR 21642], [https://github.com/django/django/pull/22007 PR 22007], [https://github.com/django/django/pull/22247 PR 22247]) are open, so I'll open it as soon as one of them is merged.

AI disclosure: I found and reproduced this with Claude Code (Anthropic).

