ticket_data.description:
pylibmc 1.8.0 from 2026-11-18 raises `ValueError: time must not be negative` during `cache.tests.PyLibMCCacheTests.test_zero_timeout`.

[https://github.com/lericson/pylibmc/releases/tag/1.8.0 Release notes] and [https://github.com/lericson/pylibmc/issues/202 PR]

Django interprets a timeout of 0 as "immediately expire". This was re-confirmed when adding the redis backend, see 7f33c1e22dbc34a7afae7967783725b10f1f13b1:

{{{#!py
        elif int(timeout) == 0:
            # Other cache backends treat 0 as set-and-expire. To achieve this
            # in memcache backends, a negative timeout must be passed.
            timeout = -1
}}}

I suggest we adapt the pylibmc backend to continue supporting this contract even when 1.8.0 is in use by adjusting it to `delete()` instead when the timeout is zero, following the example of the redis backend:

{{{#!py
        if timeout == 0:
            client.delete(key)
        else:
            client.set(key, value, ex=timeout)
}}}

This will also have the benefit of fixing the semantic when binary mode is enabled, as apparently negative values meant cache forever (and indeed this is why the `ValueError` was added to `pylibmc`).

Suggest backporting to 6.1. Another commit could make `pylibmc` 1.7 the minimum for the main branch as it's the first version to declare support for Python 3.12, but won't backport it.

raw_data.Description:
pylibmc 1.8.0 from 2026-11-18 raises `ValueError: time must not be negative` during `cache.tests.PyLibMCCacheTests.test_zero_timeout`.

[https://github.com/lericson/pylibmc/releases/tag/1.8.0 Release notes] and [https://github.com/lericson/pylibmc/issues/202 PR]

Django interprets a timeout of 0 as "immediately expire". This was re-confirmed when adding the redis backend, see 7f33c1e22dbc34a7afae7967783725b10f1f13b1:

{{{#!py
        elif int(timeout) == 0:
            # Other cache backends treat 0 as set-and-expire. To achieve this
            # in memcache backends, a negative timeout must be passed.
            timeout = -1
}}}

I suggest we adapt the pylibmc backend to continue supporting this contract even when 1.8.0 is in use by adjusting it to `delete()` instead when the timeout is zero, following the example of the redis backend:

{{{#!py
        if timeout == 0:
            client.delete(key)
        else:
            client.set(key, value, ex=timeout)
}}}

This will also have the benefit of fixing the semantic when binary mode is enabled, as apparently negative values meant cache forever (and indeed this is why the `ValueError` was added to `pylibmc`).

Suggest backporting to 6.1. Another commit could make `pylibmc` 1.7 the minimum for the main branch as it's the first version to declare support for Python 3.12, but won't backport it.

ticket_data.description:
The [https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/dev/releases/6.2/#multiline-template-tag-parsing Django 6.2 release notes] say that setting `allow_multiline_tags=False` restores the previous template parsing behavior. However, the template below compiles on Django 6.1 but fails on 6.2 even with this setting.

To reproduce on the Django 6.2 development version:

{{{
#!python
from django.template import Context, Engine

source = "{% comment %}x{%\nendcomment %}y{% endcomment %}z"
print(Engine(allow_multiline_tags=False).from_string(source).render(Context()))
}}}

Expected output: `z`. The first `endcomment` has its `{%` and `%}` on separate lines, so it should remain comment text. The final, single-line `endcomment` should close the block. On Django 6.1.3, the same template prints `z` using `Engine()` without the new option.

On `main` at `c67d872765e05ebd009fadf7de8d834b01bae9e4` (6.2 alpha 0), it raises:

{{{
TemplateSyntaxError: Invalid block tag on line 2: 'endcomment'. Did you forget to register or load this tag?
}}}

The multiline `endcomment` closes the block early, leaving the final, single-line `endcomment` outside it. Both versions were tested with Python 3.14.6.

`Lexer._tag_re_split()` uses separate patterns to match closing tags inside `comment` and `verbatim` blocks. These patterns still match across newlines when `allow_multiline_tags=False`. This also affects named and unnamed `verbatim` blocks: they end early instead of rendering the multiline lookalike as literal text.

With `allow_multiline_tags=True`, a multiline closing tag on its own ends each block as expected. #37518 concerns a missing space between `endverbatim` and the block name in the default mode.

AI disclosure: OpenAI GPT-6.2 (Astra and Sol, via OpenCode) was used to investigate and reproduce the bug.

